course: dictionary
lesson: 12104
title: A formal word with two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Cohabitation is a formal word, so you will meet it in serious writing, news and law. You do not need to know its roots to understand it. Read the context: «Their cohabitation began after they moved into one flat.» The clue is moved into one flat, so the people must be living together.
explain: The first meaning is about people. Cohabitation means living together as a couple without being married. «Many couples choose cohabitation before they decide to marry.» The clues are couples and before they decide to marry. Check by swapping in living together: the sentence still makes sense.
explain: The second meaning is about living things. Cohabitation can mean different species sharing the same place. «The cohabitation of frogs and fish in one pond surprised the scientists.» The clue is in one pond, a place where living things share a home. Ask what word would fit, and the answer is living together.
explain: In everyday talk, people usually say living together. When you meet cohabitation in a newspaper or an exam, swap in these simpler words to check your meaning.
fact: Cohabitation means living together.
fact: Cohabitation is a formal word, so it is used in serious writing, news, law and exam questions.
fact: Cohabitation can mean living together as a couple without being married.
fact: Cohabitation can also mean different living things sharing the same habitat, such as frogs and fish in one pond.
fact: Cohabitation is an uncountable noun, so you talk about cohabitation, not a cohabitation.
exercise: «Many couples choose cohabitation before they decide to marry.» What does cohabitation mean here? | Moving to a new country | Sharing a bedroom at school | Getting married in a big church | *Living together as a couple without being married
exercise: «The cohabitation of frogs and fish in one pond surprised the scientists.» What does cohabitation mean here? | *Living together in the same place | Fighting over food | Cooking for a party | Swimming in a race
exercise: Which everyday words could you swap in for cohabitation to check its meaning? | running apart | *living together | shopping trip | quiet time
test: «The rainforest is full of cohabitation between animals that share food and trees.» What does cohabitation mean? | Cutting down trees | *Living together in the same place | Being alone in the forest | Going to sleep early
test: Mia says cohabitation means a wedding. Is she right? | Yes, it means a wedding dress | No, it means living together, and that can happen without being married | Yes, it always means a wedding | *No, it means living together
exam: «Their cohabitation began in the spring.» Which phrase means the same as cohabitation here? | running away | eating dinner | *living together | going to school
